
--> Introduction to RabbitMQ
    RabbitMQ is an open-source message broker that implements the AMQP (Advanced Message Queuing Protocol).
    Why RabbitMQ?
    Enables asynchronous communication
    Decouples producer and consumer services
    Improves scalability and reliability
    Supports message acknowledgment, retry, and durability
    Real-world example
    Order Service sends an order event → Notification Service consumes it → Email/SMS is sent
    (Order service does not wait for notification service)

--> RabbitMQ Core Concepts
    Producer
        Application that sends messages
        Does not send directly to a queue
        Sends messages to an exchange
    Consumer
        Application that receives messages
        Listens to a queue
        Processes messages asynchronously
    Exchange
        Routes messages to queues
        Makes routing decisions based on:
        Exchange type
        Routing key
        Bindings
    Queue
        Stores messages until consumed
        FIFO (First In First Out)
        Can be durable or temporary
    Binding
        Connection between exchange and queue
        Defines routing rules
        Routing Key
        A string used by exchanges to route messages
        Example: order.created, payment.success
    Broker
        RabbitMQ server itself
        Manages exchanges, queues, and message delivery

--> Exchange Types
    Direct Exchange
        Routes message only if routing key exactly matches
        Used for point-to-point communication
        Example:
        routingKey = order.created
    Topic Exchange
        Uses wildcards
        * → one word
        # → zero or more words
        Best for event-based systems
        Example:
        order.* → order.created, order.updated
        order.# → all order events
    Fanout Exchange
        Broadcasts message to all bound queues
        Ignores routing key
        Example:
        Send message to:
        Email service
        SMS service
        Analytics service
    Headers Exchange
        Routes based on message headers
        Less commonly used
        Used when routing logic is complex

--> Spring Boot Integration with RabbitMQ
        Spring Boot provides spring-boot-starter-amqp to simplify RabbitMQ integration.
        Key Components
        🔹 RabbitTemplate
        Used by producer
        Sends messages to exchange
        🔹 @RabbitListener
        Used by consumer
        Listens to queues automatically
        🔹 Auto Configuration
        ConnectionFactory
        Message converters
        Listener containers

--> Advanced Tasks
        JSON Message Handling
        Use Jackson Message Converter
        Send Java Objects instead of strings

        Retry & Dead Letter Queue (DLQ)
        Retry failed messages
        After retries → move to DLQ
        Prevents message loss

        Topic & Fanout Use Cases
        Topic → event-driven microservices
        Fanout → notifications, logs, analytics

        Microservices Integration Scenario
        Example Architecture:
        Order Service → RabbitMQ
        Inventory Service
        Payment Service
        Notification Service
        Each service reacts independently.
